/-! # Reconstruction from primitive functions and Frobenius

Source: Milne, Algebraic Groups (2017), Theorem 14.46, printed p.295,
with Proposition 14.41, Remark 14.42 and Lemma 14.43, printed pp.293--294.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-primitive-frobenius-module-duality)
-/

set_option autoImplicit false

@[expose] public section

universe u v

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits
open scoped CategoryTheory.MonObj

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

open FrobeniusPrimitive

/-- Finite Frobenius skew modules are represented by finite-type closed additive subgroups,
and evaluation reconstructs every such subgroup from its actual primitive functions.

Source: Milne, Proposition 14.41, Remark 14.42 and Theorem 14.46, pp.293--295.
(blueprint: lem-sfb-primitive-frobenius-module-duality)

`Representation` includes natural additive bijections on every test algebra. The second
clause specifies the evaluation isomorphism for any such representer of P(G), so the
result does not depend on a choice of representing object. A positive characteristic
of a field is prime; `Fact p.Prime` states this same characteristic condition.

Proof:
1. For an affine group $G=\operatorname{Spec}C$, identify a morphism $f:G\to\mathbf G_a$ with
$a=f^*(t)\in C$. By the coordinate Hopf identities and $\Delta(t)=t\otimes1+1\otimes t$, it is a
group homomorphism exactly when $\Delta_C(a)=a\otimes1+1\otimes a$. This equation forces
$\epsilon_C(a)=0$ and $S_C(a)=-a$ by applying the counit and antipode identities. Thus $P(G)$ is
precisely this linear space of primitive elements. Frobenius additivity shows that $a\mapsto a^p$
preserves it and satisfies $F(ca)=c^pF(a)$. The rule $(cF^i)a=ca^{p^i}$ gives the stated left
$A$-action, since composition of these operators has the displayed skew multiplication.
Dependencies: def-weil-affine-spectrum, def-sc-gm-ga, lem-sfb-affine-group-coordinate-hopf,
def-weil-ring-frobenius.

2. For any left $A$-module $V$, not yet assumed finitely generated, put $T(v)=Fv$ and construct
$B(V,T)$ and $j$ as in the basis node. The symmetric-algebra universal property followed by the
quotient property gives, naturally for every commutative $k$-algebra $R$, a bijection
$$\operatorname{Hom}_{k\text{-alg}}(B(V,T),R)=\{h:V\to R\text{
linear}:h(Tv)=h(v)^p\}=\operatorname{Hom}_A(V,R).$$ Indeed both sides send $j(v)$ to $h(v)$;
respecting $k$ and $F$ is exactly respecting every finite sum in $A$. This also proves uniqueness
of all algebra maps out of $B(V,T)$ from their values on $j(V)$.
Dependencies: lem-sfb-semilinear-restricted-symmetric-basis,
lem-weil-pbc-symmetric-algebra-universal-property, def-weil-pbc-algebra-hom-quotient.

3. Define algebra maps on $B=B(V,T)$ by $$\Delta(jv)=jv\otimes1+1\otimes
jv,\qquad\epsilon(jv)=0,\qquad S(jv)=-jv.$$ They exist by step 2: for $\Delta$ the $p$th-power
equation is $(jv\otimes1+1\otimes jv)^p=j(Tv)\otimes1+1\otimes j(Tv)$; the other two equations are
immediate, including $p=2$. The tensor-product algebra structure is the cited one.
Coassociativity, the two counit identities, the two antipode identities and symmetry of $\Delta$
hold on each $jv$, hence on $B$ by uniqueness in step 2. The Hopf-spectrum functor therefore
supplies an affine commutative group $\operatorname{Spec}B$ representing the functor in step 2.
The displayed comultiplication induces pointwise addition on $\operatorname{Hom}_A(V,R)$.
Dependencies: def-weil-ring-frobenius, lem-weil-pbc-algebra-tensor-pushout-universal-property,
lem-sfb-hopf-spectrum-functor.

4. If $M$ has $A$-generators $m_1,\ldots,m_r$, every $j(m)$ is a linear combination of
$j(F^im_s)=j(m_s)^{p^i}$. Since $B(M,F)$ is generated by $j(M)$, it is generated as a $k$-algebra
by the finite list $j(m_s)$. Hence it is a quotient of $k[t_1,\ldots,t_r]$, and its spectrum is a
closed subscheme of $\mathbf G_a^r$. This is a group embedding because each coordinate $j(m_s)$ is
primitive. The finite affine polynomial presentation makes its structure morphism finite type
directly from the finite-affine-cover definition. This proves all the representability,
commutativity, finite-type and embedding assertions for $U(M)$, including $M=0$ and $r=0$.
Dependencies: def-weil-finitely-generated-module, def-weil-pbc-finite-type-algebra,
def-weil-closed-immersion, def-weil-finite-type-morphism, def-sc-gm-ga.

5. Choose a $k$-basis $(e_i)$ of $V$. The basis node gives linearly independent monomials
$b_a=\prod j(e_i)^{a_i}$ with $0\le a_i<p$. Multiplicativity of $\Delta$ gives
$$\Delta(b_a)=\sum_{r+s=a}\left(\prod_i\binom{a_i}{r_i}\right)b_r\otimes b_s.$$ For every $a$ with
$\sum a_i\ge2$, choose $i$ with $a_i>0$ and split $a=\delta_i+(a-\delta_i)$. Both terms are
nonzero, and the coefficient of $b_{\delta_i}\otimes b_{a-\delta_i}$ in a linear combination's
reduced coproduct is exactly $a_i$ times its $b_a$ coefficient: no other monomial can contribute
to this tensor. Since $1\le a_i<p$, it is nonzero in $k$. Thus every primitive is in $j(V)$; its
constant coefficient is zero by the counit. Conversely all $j(v)$ are primitive. Therefore $j$
identifies $V$ with the primitive space of $B(V,T)$. The subspaces spanned by $b_a$ with $\sum
a_i\le n$ are exhaustive, start with $k1$, and satisfy the coalgebra filtration condition, by the
same formula.
Dependencies: lem-sfb-semilinear-restricted-symmetric-basis, lem-weil-pbc-vector-space-free.

6. Let $C$ be a commutative Hopf $k$-algebra and let $V\subseteq C$ be a linear subspace of
primitive elements stable under $p$th powers. Step 2 extends the inclusion to an algebra map
$q:B(V,F)\to C$. The formulas on generators show that it commutes with comultiplication and
counit. If it were not injective, the connected-filtered-coalgebra kernel lemma, applied to the
filtration in step 5 with distinguished element $1$, would give a nonzero primitive element in its
kernel. Step 5 says this element is $j(v)$, and then $q(j(v))=v$ forces $v=0$, a contradiction to
injectivity of $j$. Thus $q$ is injective. Notice that neither $V$ nor $C$ needs a
finite-generation assumption here.
Dependencies: lem-sfb-connected-filtered-coalgebra-kernel-primitive.

7. Now fix a closed group embedding $G\hookrightarrow\mathbf G_a^r$ and write $C=k[G]$. The
coordinate map $k[t_1,\ldots,t_r]\twoheadrightarrow C$ is surjective; let $x_s$ be the images of
the coordinates. They are primitive by step 1. Let $N$ be their left $A$-span inside
$P(G)\subseteq C$. Then $N$ is generated by $x_1,\ldots,x_r$ as an $A$-module. Step 6 makes
$q:B(N,F)\to C$ injective; it is also surjective since the $x_s$ generate $C$ as an algebra. This
algebra isomorphism respects the Hopf maps, so it identifies their primitive spaces. Step 5
identifies the primitive space of $B(N,F)$ with $N$, and $q$ is the inclusion on $N$. Consequently
$P(G)=N$, proving finite generation without presupposing it for the construction of $B(P(G),F)$.
Dependencies: def-weil-closed-immersion, def-sc-gm-ga.

8. Since $P(G)=N$, the isomorphism of step 7 is exactly the map $B(P(G),F)\to C$ sending $j(f)$ to
the coordinate function $f$. Taking spectra gives $G\simeq U(P(G))$. For any commutative
$k$-algebra $R$, a point $g:C\to R$ is sent to the $A$-linear map $f\mapsto g(f)=f_R(g)$. The
formula is natural in $R$, and preserves addition since every $f$ is a group homomorphism. It is
therefore the asserted evaluation isomorphism with its specified value on all $R$-points,
including nonreduced algebras.
Dependencies: def-weil-affine-spectrum.
-/
theorem primitive_frobenius_module_duality
    {k : Type u} [Field k] (p : ℕ) [Fact p.Prime] [CharP k p] :
    (∀ (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module (SkewRing k p) M]
      [Module.Finite (SkewRing k p) M],
      ∃ U : Representation k p M,
        IsAffine U.group.X.left ∧
        LocallyOfFiniteType U.group.X.hom ∧ QuasiCompact U.group.X.hom ∧
        ∃ (n : ℕ)
          (i : U.group ⟶ ∏ᶜ fun _ : Fin n ↦ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.additive k)),
          IsClosedSubgroupScheme i.hom.hom) ∧
    (∀ (G : Grp (Over (Spec (.of k))))
      [IsAffine G.X.left] [LocallyOfFiniteType G.X.hom] [QuasiCompact G.X.hom],
      (∃ (n : ℕ)
        (i : G ⟶ ∏ᶜ fun _ : Fin n ↦ Grp.mk (StandardGroupSchemes.additive k)),
        IsClosedSubgroupScheme i.hom.hom) →
      Module.Finite (SkewRing k p) (PrimitiveFunctions k G) ∧
        ∀ U : Representation k p (PrimitiveFunctions k G),
          ∃ e : G ≅ U.group,
            ∀ (R : Type u) [CommRing R] [Algebra k R]
              (g : Points k G.X R) (f : PrimitiveFunctions k G),
              U.points R (Additive.ofMul (g ≫ e.hom.hom.hom)) f =
                primitiveValue k G R f g) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
